dence, can flip. Prior work documents this brittleness as a failure of robust knowledge. We reframe it mechanistically: we hypothesize that when an LLM ingests a novel fact stated in a particular surface form, it updates an internal belief that is *bound to that form* rather than to a canonical meaning-level representation. Under this view, two paraphrases of the same proposition instantiate two distinct internal belief handles that can be updated—and even contradicted—independently. We test this with a controlled in-context belief-injection paradigm. We construct synthetic facts (novel entity-attribute pairs, unlikely to be in pretraining) rendered in matched surface variants (paraphrases, syntactic reorderings, and translations). We inject a fact in form A and then probe the internal belief when the model reads the same proposition in form B, quantifying cross-form belief transfer with linear belief probes on frozen open-weight models (Llama-3, Qwen2.5, Gemma-2). We predict transfer is high within form and degrades systematically with surface distance, tracing a layerwise 'binding site' where form-specific and meaning-level representations converge. We then perform the critical causal test: inject *contradictory* facts under two forms and measure whether the model maintains two coexisting internal beliefs (form-bound) or resolves them (meaning-bound), and whether steering along a 'form-invariance' direction (derived contrastively from high- vs. low-transfer pairs) increases cross-form belief consistency without harming fluency. Metrics: cross-form transfer curves, layerwise binding-site localization via activation patching, contradiction-coexistence rate, post-steering consistency gains, and perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset: Generate ~4k novel synthetic facts (invented entities with attributes, e.g., 'Zorblax-9 orbits the star Kelvath') to control for pretraining priors. For each fact, produce matched surface variants: (a) near-paraphrase, (b) syntactic reordering (active/passive, subject/object swap of a symmetric-looking relation), (c) lexical substitution, (d) translation into 2 languages. Include true/false polarity and a control set of well-known real facts. 2) Belief probes: On frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B), extract residual-stream activations at the attribute/answer token and train linear belief probes (P(fact holds)) with shuffled-label control tasks. 3) Cross-form belief injection: Present a fact in form A in context, then read the internal belief for the same proposition presented in form B (query). Measure cross-form transfer = probe belief in B given A-injection, minus baseline. Build a transfer matrix across all form pairs and correlate transfer with surface-distance metrics (edit distance, embedding cosine). 4) Binding-site localization: Use activation patching to find the layer(s) where a fact injected in form A begins to influence the form-B representation (patch A-context activations into a B-only run; find minimal set restoring belief). Compare early (form-specific) vs. later (meaning-level) convergence, mapping the 'binding site'. 5) Contradiction test (critical): Inject contradictory facts under two forms (A asserts, B negates) and measure whether internal beliefs coexist (both probes read their injected value → form-bound) or collapse to one (→ meaning-bound); report a contradiction-coexistence rate per layer and behaviorally via direct queries. 6) Form-invariance steering: Derive a steering direction via contrastive activation addition from high-transfer vs. low-transfer form pairs; apply during B-query and measure (a) increased cross-form belief consistency, (b) contradiction resolution, (c) collateral effects on unrelated facts and general benchmarks (MMLU subset), (d) perplexity. 7) Generalization: Test transfer of probes and steering vectors across fact domains and to real-fact and cross-lingual subsets. Metrics: cross-form transfer curves and matrices, surface-distance correlations, patching effect sizes / binding-site layer, contradiction-coexistence rate, post-steering consistency delta, perplexity delta.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "Cross-form transfer failures could reflect comprehension failures rather than form-bound belief storage; we include comprehension checks and use matched real-fact controls. Synthetic entities may be represented differently from real knowledge; we complement with real-fact subsets. Linear belief probes may not cleanly capture the belief at every layer; we report layerwise results and use control tasks. The 'binding site' may be distributed rather than a discrete layer, weakening the clean localization story (still informative as a gradient). Form-invariance steering may degrade fluency or overwrite legitimate distinctions (e.g., subject/object asymmetric relations); we monitor perplexity, use small magnitudes, and separately analyze symmetric vs. asymmetric relations. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}

