Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "belief_ripple_internal", "Title": "Does the Ripple Happen Inside? Probing Whether LLMs Internally Propagate In-Context Belief Updates Before Being Asked", "Short Hypothesis": "When an LLM reads a new fact stated in context, it internally updates its belief not only about that fact but, silently and before any query, about logically-entailed related facts. This internal 'ripple' is linearly decodable at the entailed-item representation, is graded and selective (some entailments propagate, others don't), and its magnitude at read-time predicts which downstream ripple queries the model will answer correctly. Ripple failures are thus visible internally at ingestion time, not just at output time.", "Related Work": "Ripple-effect work (RippleEdits 2023; ChainEdit 2025; PropMEND 2025; GradSim 2024; GIE/SIR 2024; KnowledgeSmith 2025; TRACK 2026) studies whether *edited* or in-context knowledge propagates to related facts, but measures propagation only behaviorally (multi-hop QA accuracy) or via gradient/hidden-space edit-side diagnostics; all require *querying* the related fact. 'Believe It or Not' (2025) and 'Do LMs Have Beliefs?' (2021) probe belief depth/consistency of implanted facts but again read beliefs by querying. None ask whether, upon merely *reading* a fact in context (no edit, no query), the model *spontaneously* updates the internal representation of logically-entailed items, whether that update is decodable at read-time, graded/selective per entailment type, and predictive of ripple success. We reframe the ripple as an internal, read-time representational-propagation phenomenon and provide a linear-probe + activation-patching handle on it, distinct from all output-level and edit-side prior work.", "Abstract": "Telling a language model 'Jack is Johnny's son' should, if the model reasons coherently, make it also believe 'Jack is Lily-Rose's sibling.' Prior work shows this ripple often fails, but always measures it by *asking* the related question. We ask a sharper, mechanistic question: when the model merely *reads* a new fact in context, does it silently update its internal belief about logically-entailed facts, before any query? We hypothesize that (i) such internal ripple updates are linearly decodable from residual-stream activations at the entailed item's representation, (ii) they are graded and selective across entailment types (e.g., symmetric relations, inverse relations, transitive/multi-hop, type-inheritance), and (iii) the read-time magnitude of the internal update predicts whether the model will answer the corresponding ripple query correctly. We construct controlled synthetic entity-relation graphs with novel entities (to bypass pretraining priors) plus a real-fact track. For each injected base fact, we enumerate entailed facts of known types and measure the internal belief about each entailed fact both before and after the base fact appears in context. We train linear belief probes on frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Pythia-2.8B), quantify per-entailment-type internal ripple magnitude, test whether it predicts downstream ripple-QA accuracy (AUROC) beyond a log-prob baseline, and localize the heads that carry propagation via activation patching. Finally, we test causal amplification: steering along a 'propagation' direction (contrastive activation addition from strong- vs. weak-ripple cases) to see if it increases both internal ripple magnitude and ripple-QA accuracy. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal-ripple magnitude by entailment type, ripple-success prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering accuracy delta, perplexity.", "Experiments": "1) Dataset construction: Build controlled synthetic entity-relation graphs with novel entities (e.g., invented people, places, companies) so entailed facts are not in pretraining. For each graph, designate a BASE fact to inject in context (e.g., 'Zorbot is the founder of Kelvara') and enumerate ENTAILED facts by type: (a) symmetric ('Kelvara was founded by Zorbot'), (b) inverse-relation ('Kelvara's founder is Zorbot' vs. 'Zorbot's company is Kelvara'), (c) transitive/multi-hop ('Kelvara is headquartered in city X' -> 'Zorbot works in X'), (d) type-inheritance ('Zorbot is a person' -> has properties of persons), and (e) NON-entailed distractor facts (should NOT ripple). Include a real-fact track adapted from RippleEdits for external validity. For each entailed/distractor fact, prepare a matched no-base-context control (fact never introduced). ~5k base facts with several entailments each. 2) Read-time internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Pythia-2.8B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the entailed-item's key tokens, in two conditions: (BASE-present) the base fact appears earlier in context, and (BASE-absent) it does not, WITHOUT ever asking the entailed question. Train linear belief probes (P(entailed fact holds)) on held-out data with shuffled-label control tasks. Define internal-ripple magnitude = probe belief(BASE-present) - probe belief(BASE-absent) at the entailed item, per layer, per entailment type. 3) Selectivity analysis: Test whether internal ripple magnitude is high for genuine entailments and near-zero for non-entailed distractors, and how it varies across entailment types (symmetric/inverse/transitive/inheritance). Report per-type ripple magnitude curves across layers. 4) Predictiveness: Separately query each entailed fact behaviorally (ripple-QA) to get ground-truth ripple success/failure. Test whether read-time internal-ripple magnitude predicts per-item ripple-QA success (AUROC), compared against a log-prob confidence baseline and against GradSim-style edit-side indicators adapted to in-context. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from BASE-present to BASE-absent runs to find the heads/layers that carry propagation to the entailed item; test whether distinct heads mediate different entailment types (e.g., symmetric vs. multi-hop). 6) Causal amplification: Derive a 'propagation' steering direction via contrastive activation addition from strong-ripple vs. weak-ripple base-present activations; inject it during reading and measure (a) increased internal ripple magnitude at entailed items, (b) increased ripple-QA accuracy, (c) that non-entailed distractors are NOT spuriously updated (specificity), (d) collateral effects on general benchmarks (MMLU subset) and perplexity. Compare against random-direction steering. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across entailment types, from synthetic to real-fact track, and across graph depths (1-hop vs. multi-hop). Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal-ripple magnitude by entailment type and layer, ripple-success prediction AUROC, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering accuracy and specificity deltas, perplexity delta.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "Read-time internal ripple may be weak or absent (models may only compute entailments when queried), in which case a robust *negative* result is still highly informative for the ripple/editing literature and reframes ripple failures as a lack of spontaneous propagation. The entailed-item probe signal could reflect surface co-occurrence of tokens rather than a genuine belief update; matched BASE-absent controls, non-entailed distractors, and paraphrase perturbations mitigate this. Multi-hop entailments may not have a single clean probe target; we anchor probes at well-defined key tokens and report per-type results. Ripple magnitude and ripple-QA success could both be driven by a common confound (fact salience); we control salience and use partial-correlation analyses. Steering could degrade fluency or spuriously propagate to distractors; we monitor perplexity, specificity on distractors, and general benchmarks, and use small magnitudes with random-direction baselines. Synthetic novel entities may be represented differently from real knowledge; the real-fact track provides a check. Results may not transfer to very large closed models."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'belief_ripple_internal', 'Title': 'Does the Ripple Happen Inside? Probing Whether LLMs Internally Propagate In-Context Belief Updates Before Being Asked', 'Short Hypothesis': "When an LLM reads a new fact stated in context, it internally updates its belief not only about that fact but, silently and before any query, about logically-entailed related facts. This internal 'ripple' is linearly decodable at the entailed-item representation, is graded and selective (some entailments propagate, others don't), and its magnitude at read-time predicts which downstream ripple queries the model will answer correctly. Ripple failures are thus visible internally at ingestion time, not just at output time.", 'Related Work': "Ripple-effect work (RippleEdits 2023; ChainEdit 2025; PropMEND 2025; GradSim 2024; GIE/SIR 2024; KnowledgeSmith 2025; TRACK 2026) studies whether *edited* or in-context knowledge propagates to related facts, but measures propagation only behaviorally (multi-hop QA accuracy) or via gradient/hidden-space edit-side diagnostics; all require *querying* the related fact. 'Believe It or Not' (2025) and 'Do LMs Have Beliefs?' (2021) probe belief depth/consistency of implanted facts but again read beliefs by querying. None ask whether, upon merely *reading* a fact in context (no edit, no query), the model *spontaneously* updates the internal representation of logically-entailed items, whether that update is decodable at read-time, graded/selective per entailment type, and predictive of ripple success. We reframe the ripple as an internal, read-time representational-propagation phenomenon and provide a linear-probe + activation-patching handle on it, distinct from all output-level and edit-side prior work.", 'Abstract': "Telling a language model 'Jack is Johnny's son' should, if the model reasons coherently, make it also believe 'Jack is Lily-Rose's sibling.' Prior work shows this ripple often fails, but always measures it by *asking* the related question. We ask a sharper, mechanistic question: when the model merely *reads* a new fact in context, does it silently update its internal belief about logically-entailed facts, before any query? We hypothesize that (i) such internal ripple updates are linearly decodable from residual-stream activations at the entailed item's representation, (ii) they are graded and selective across entailment types (e.g., symmetric relations, inverse relations, transitive/multi-hop, type-inheritance), and (iii) the read-time magnitude of the internal update predicts whether the model will answer the corresponding ripple query correctly. We construct controlled synthetic entity-relation graphs with novel entities (to bypass pretraining priors) plus a real-fact track. For each injected base fact, we enumerate entailed facts of known types and measure the internal belief about each entailed fact both before and after the base fact appears in context. We train linear belief probes on frozen open-weight models (Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Pythia-2.8B), quantify per-entailment-type internal ripple magnitude, test whether it predicts downstream ripple-QA accuracy (AUROC) beyond a log-prob baseline, and localize the heads that carry propagation via activation patching. Finally, we test causal amplification: steering along a 'propagation' direction (contrastive activation addition from strong- vs. weak-ripple cases) to see if it increases both internal ripple magnitude and ripple-QA accuracy. Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal-ripple magnitude by entailment type, ripple-success prediction AUROC, patching effect sizes, post-steering accuracy delta, perplexity.", 'Experiments': "1) Dataset construction: Build controlled synthetic entity-relation graphs with novel entities (e.g., invented people, places, companies) so entailed facts are not in pretraining. For each graph, designate a BASE fact to inject in context (e.g., 'Zorbot is the founder of Kelvara') and enumerate ENTAILED facts by type: (a) symmetric ('Kelvara was founded by Zorbot'), (b) inverse-relation ('Kelvara's founder is Zorbot' vs. 'Zorbot's company is Kelvara'), (c) transitive/multi-hop ('Kelvara is headquartered in city X' -> 'Zorbot works in X'), (d) type-inheritance ('Zorbot is a person' -> has properties of persons), and (e) NON-entailed distractor facts (should NOT ripple). Include a real-fact track adapted from RippleEdits for external validity. For each entailed/distractor fact, prepare a matched no-base-context control (fact never introduced). ~5k base facts with several entailments each. 2) Read-time internal belief probing: For Llama-3-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Pythia-2.8B, extract residual-stream and per-head activations at the entailed-item's key tokens, in two conditions: (BASE-present) the base fact appears earlier in context, and (BASE-absent) it does not, WITHOUT ever asking the entailed question. Train linear belief probes (P(entailed fact holds)) on held-out data with shuffled-label control tasks. Define internal-ripple magnitude = probe belief(BASE-present) - probe belief(BASE-absent) at the entailed item, per layer, per entailment type. 3) Selectivity analysis: Test whether internal ripple magnitude is high for genuine entailments and near-zero for non-entailed distractors, and how it varies across entailment types (symmetric/inverse/transitive/inheritance). Report per-type ripple magnitude curves across layers. 4) Predictiveness: Separately query each entailed fact behaviorally (ripple-QA) to get ground-truth ripple success/failure. Test whether read-time internal-ripple magnitude predicts per-item ripple-QA success (AUROC), compared against a log-prob confidence baseline and against GradSim-style edit-side indicators adapted to in-context. 5) Localization: Use activation patching / causal mediation from BASE-present to BASE-absent runs to find the heads/layers that carry propagation to the entailed item; test whether distinct heads mediate different entailment types (e.g., symmetric vs. multi-hop). 6) Causal amplification: Derive a 'propagation' steering direction via contrastive activation addition from strong-ripple vs. weak-ripple base-present activations; inject it during reading and measure (a) increased internal ripple magnitude at entailed items, (b) increased ripple-QA accuracy, (c) that non-entailed distractors are NOT spuriously updated (specificity), (d) collateral effects on general benchmarks (MMLU subset) and perplexity. Compare against random-direction steering. 7) Generalization: Test probe/steering transfer across entailment types, from synthetic to real-fact track, and across graph depths (1-hop vs. multi-hop). Metrics: probe accuracy/AUROC, internal-ripple magnitude by entailment type and layer, ripple-success prediction AUROC, patching logit-difference effect sizes, post-steering accuracy and specificity deltas, perplexity delta.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': 'Read-time internal ripple may be weak or absent (models may only compute entailments when queried), in which case a robust *negative* result is still highly informative for the ripple/editing literature and reframes ripple failures as a lack of spontaneous propagation. The entailed-item probe signal could reflect surface co-occurrence of tokens rather than a genuine belief update; matched BASE-absent controls, non-entailed distractors, and paraphrase perturbations mitigate this. Multi-hop entailments may not have a single clean probe target; we anchor probes at well-defined key tokens and report per-type results. Ripple magnitude and ripple-QA success could both be driven by a common confound (fact salience); we control salience and use partial-correlation analyses. Steering could degrade fluency or spuriously propagate to distractors; we monitor perplexity, specificity on distractors, and general benchmarks, and use small magnitudes with random-direction baselines. Synthetic novel entities may be represented differently from real knowledge; the real-fact track provides a check. Results may not transfer to very large closed models.'}

